  "name": "Ghināʾ laḥjī (Lahji song)",
  "family": "global",
  "lineage": "Song school of Lahij, north of Aden, shaped in the early twentieth century by the poet-composer Ahmed Fadl al-Qumandan of the Lahij sultanate, who set colloquial poetry to the melodies and rhythms of the surrounding countryside. Later Lahji singers such as Faisal Alawi carried it through radio, records and cassettes, singing to the oud with violins and hand drums; the fiddle card stands for the violins.",
